  "text": "Question two. What do ref and source do? Source points at a raw table that something else loaded, declared once by name. Ref points at another model. Here the staging model reads raw orders through source, and the orders fact reads the staging model through ref. Each call tells dbt that one thing depends on another. Hardcode the table name instead, and dbt no longer sees that dependency. Nothing stops it building the fact before the model it reads, and the lineage graph has a gap where the edge was. Question three. What is the dbt lineage dag, and how is it built? Dag is short for directed acyclic graph. dbt parses every ref and source call in the project, and each call becomes an edge, from the node it names to the model that names it. The page's slice has two sources, four models and five edges. Both staging models depend only on sources, so they build side by side. The customer dimension waits for its staging model, and the orders fact for both branches: four models in three steps. Acyclic is what makes an order possible. If two models referenced each other, neither could go first.",
